{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.1.3","title":"Require hosted Playwright success in the aggregate CI gate","description":"Validation enabler discovered while integrating panel acceptance criteria. The repository already has a separate `ui-playwright` job that installs the pinned pnpm 12.9.1 dependencies and browser and runs `pnpm run test:e2e`; however, the aggregate `test` job did not depend on that result. Phase 1 adds the dependency and fails closed unless the job succeeds. The protected main branch at base d4bc8e7 requires the aggregate `test` and `codeql`; `test` depends on `ui-playwright`, so browser validation is transitively required. This issue is closed: hosted same-head Playwright and aggregate checks passed in CI run 37419778717 on code head a334cf900b61f5273d4005a05cdf71b79e7cc6b7.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [x] The aggregate `test` job depends on `ui-playwright` and fails if its result is failure, cancelled, or skipped while applicable.\n- [x] The workflow contract asserts both the dependency and fail-closed result semantics. A future job condition must define applicability and cannot silently skip a relevant UI PR test.\n- [x] Hosted Playwright and aggregate CI passed on the same head in run 37419778717 (code head a334cf900b61f5273d4005a05cdf71b79e7cc6b7).\n- [x] Verified branch protection at base d4bc8e7: protected `main` requires aggregate `test` and `codeql`; `test` requires `ui-playwright` transitively, not as a separate context.\n\n## Test Plan\n- Run the CI workflow contract and actionlint.\n- Run `pnpm run test:e2e` locally when the environment permits the configured web-server bind.\n- Verify hosted success and artifacts on a UI PR.\n- Branch API confirmed required checks (`test`, `codeql`) on protected `main`; verify hosted same-head job results remain green.\n\n## Scope\nThis is a CI validation enabler, not a finding from the UX panel. Do not change app product behavior in this issue.","acceptance_criteria":"- The aggregate test job depends on ui-playwright and fails on a failed or skipped applicable result. - Workflow contract tests assert the dependency and fail-closed behavior. - Hosted Playwright and aggregate CI passed on code head a334cf900b61f5273d4005a05cdf71b79e7cc6b7 in run 37419778717. - Branch-protection required-check status is verified before claiming it is required.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#hosted-playwright-validation-gate","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"chore","labels":["ci","frontend","milestone","pr-1","review-remediation","ux","ux-validation"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.1.3","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.1","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.4.4","title":"Expose admin, baseline, import, and reset outcomes as accessible statuses","description":"Finding: UX-10 from the 2026-10-05 frontend UX adversarial panel.\nSource: Review report UX-10; ui/src/admin-bridge-panel.tsx:34-36; ui/src/App.tsx:190-213,463-490,544-546.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n1. Trigger a successful Apply/Reset and an admin/baseline result. 2. Trigger invalid JSON and a failed bridge operation. 3. Observe status semantics, focus, and field association.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Success and asynchronous outcomes use appropriate role=status/live feedback without unexpectedly moving focus.\n- [ ] Errors use alert semantics when urgent and are programmatically associated with the relevant input.\n- [ ] The payload textarea exposes invalid state and its help/error text through accessible relationships.\n- [ ] Repeated updates do not create duplicate or stale announcements.\n\n## Test Plan\nComponent-test role=status, alert conditions, aria-invalid and describedby relationships. Playwright verifies updates after successful/failed bridge stubs. Manually check announcements with at least one screen reader and keyboard-only flow. Run UI typecheck, Vitest, build, and Playwright.\n\n## Scope and delivery\nThis item applies only to the React/Vite browser preview unless the scope decision explicitly creates a separate item. Preserve native Rust shell contracts. Implement under parent milestone BI-5u3.4; collect evidence before marking complete.\n","acceptance_criteria":"- Success and asynchronous outcomes use appropriate role=status/live feedback without unexpectedly moving focus.\n- Errors use alert semantics when urgent and are programmatically associated with the relevant input.\n- The payload textarea exposes invalid state and its help/error text through accessible relationships.\n- Repeated updates do not create duplicate or stale announcements.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#ux-10","status":"open","priority":2,"issue_type":"bug","labels":["frontend","m3","milestone","review-panel","review-remediation","ux","ux-10"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.4.4","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.4","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.4.3","title":"Name the Field/Lab switch input accessibly","description":"Finding: UX-09 from the 2026-10-05 frontend UX adversarial panel.\nSource: Review report UX-09; ui/src/App.tsx:790-798; installed MUI 9.4 Switch/SwitchBase prop forwarding.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n1. Inspect the rendered switch input's accessible name and role. 2. Navigate to it with keyboard and toggle with Space. 3. Confirm state and any displayed data/label update.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The actual interactive input with role=switch exposes a stable, meaningful accessible name.\n- [ ] Visible Field Data/Lab Data text is associated with the input or the input has a suitable input-slot name.\n- [ ] Checked state communicates which data mode is active; if the switch does not change data, UX-03's preview-only explanation is also visible.\n- [ ] Tests use role/name queries against the interactive input, not a wrapper selector.\n\n## Test Plan\nUse Testing Library getByRole('switch',{name:...}) and assert checked state/toggle behavior. Inspect the browser accessibility tree and perform a keyboard/assistive-technology spot check. Run UI typecheck, Vitest, build, and Playwright.\n\n## Scope and delivery\nThis item applies only to the React/Vite browser preview unless the scope decision explicitly creates a separate item. Preserve native Rust shell contracts. Implement under parent milestone BI-5u3.4; collect evidence before marking complete.\n","acceptance_criteria":"- The actual interactive input with role=switch exposes a stable, meaningful accessible name.\n- Visible Field Data/Lab Data text is associated with the input or the input has a suitable input-slot name.\n- Checked state communicates which data mode is active; if the switch does not change data, UX-03's preview-only explanation is also visible.\n- Tests use role/name queries against the interactive input, not a wrapper selector.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#ux-09","status":"open","priority":2,"issue_type":"bug","labels":["frontend","m3","milestone","review-panel","review-remediation","ux","ux-09"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.4.3","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.4","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.4.2","title":"Meet WCAG text contrast for primary buttons and states","description":"Finding: UX-08 from the 2026-10-05 frontend UX adversarial panel.\nSource: Review report UX-08; ui/src/main.tsx:6-12; ui/src/App.tsx:480,705-714; WCAG 2.2 SC 1.4.3.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n1. Render enabled contained and outlined primary buttons with default theme. 2. Measure foreground/background contrast at default, hover, and focus states. 3. Include small button text.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Enabled normal-size button text meets at least 4.5:1 in contained and outlined default states.\n- [ ] Large text, if any, meets the applicable 3:1 threshold; do not use this exception for 13-14px button text.\n- [ ] Hover, keyboard focus, disabled, and theme variants are measured; disabled-state claims are reported separately where WCAG exemptions apply.\n- [ ] Contrast values and color token decisions are documented in a repeatable UI check.\n\n## Test Plan\nAdd a deterministic contrast-unit check for theme color tokens and a browser computed-style check for rendered contained/outlined buttons. Manually inspect hover/focus/disabled states. Run UI typecheck, Vitest, build, and Playwright. Target applies to the measured 4.2884:1 default pair.\n\n## Scope and delivery\nThis item applies only to the React/Vite browser preview unless the scope decision explicitly creates a separate item. Preserve native Rust shell contracts. Implement under parent milestone BI-5u3.4; collect evidence before marking complete.\n","acceptance_criteria":"- Enabled normal-size button text meets at least 4.5:1 in contained and outlined default states.\n- Large text, if any, meets the applicable 3:1 threshold; do not use this exception for 13-14px button text.\n- Hover, keyboard focus, disabled, and theme variants are measured; disabled-state claims are reported separately where WCAG exemptions apply.\n- Contrast values and color token decisions are documented in a repeatable UI check.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#ux-08","status":"open","priority":2,"issue_type":"bug","labels":["frontend","m3","milestone","review-panel","review-remediation","ux","ux-08"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.4.2","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.4","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.4.1","title":"Make audit, SEO, and Field/Lab controls honest about available behavior","description":"Finding: UX-03 from the 2026-10-05 frontend UX adversarial panel.\nSource: Review report UX-03; ui/src/App.tsx:705-714,733-745,787-798.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] No enabled control silently does nothing.\n- [ ] Each control either changes the represented data/starts an operation or is clearly disabled/labeled as unavailable or illustrative in this preview.\n- [ ] SEO tabs alter the corresponding findings/metrics, or explicitly explain why displayed guidance remains shared.\n- [ ] Field/Lab toggling changes source data only if distinct datasets exist; otherwise the control is not presented as a functional selector.\n- [ ] No change implies new native-shell behavior or alters command payload contracts.\n\n## Test Plan\nVitest/Testing Library tests each control's enabled state, action, and visible outcome. Playwright exercises pointer and keyboard activation. Run UI typecheck, Vitest, production build, and Playwright. If behavior is intentionally illustrative, assert the explanatory label.\n\n## Scope and delivery\nThis item applies only to the React/Vite browser preview unless the scope decision explicitly creates a separate item. Preserve native Rust shell contracts. Implement under parent milestone BI-5u3.4; collect evidence before marking complete.\n","acceptance_criteria":"- No enabled control silently does nothing.\n- Each control either changes the represented data/starts an operation or is clearly disabled/labeled as unavailable or illustrative in this preview.\n- SEO tabs alter the corresponding findings/metrics, or explicitly explain why displayed guidance remains shared.\n- Field/Lab toggling changes source data only if distinct datasets exist; otherwise the control is not presented as a functional selector.\n- No change implies new native-shell behavior or alters command payload contracts.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#ux-03","status":"open","priority":2,"issue_type":"task","labels":["frontend","m3","milestone","review-panel","review-remediation","ux","ux-03"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.4.1","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.4","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.3.4","title":"Make score explanation match the score formula","description":"Finding: UX-07 from the 2026-10-05 frontend UX adversarial panel.\nSource: Review report UX-07; ui/src/dashboard-explainability.ts:35-43; ui/src/domain/quality-pulse.ts:163-180; ui/src/App.tsx:165,352,407.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n1. Keep commits and stages fixed. 2. Change only opportunity signals. 3. Compare overall score and explainability text.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The UI never says opportunity signals raise the score unless the formula actually uses them.\n- [ ] The formula and explanation share a single source of truth for scoring components.\n- [ ] An opportunity-only change cannot create a score-contribution claim without a corresponding score delta.\n- [ ] If a product decision adds opportunities to scoring, the weight and resulting behavior are documented and regression-tested.\n\n## Test Plan\nUnit-test score calculations with commits/stages held constant and opportunities changed. Test the explanation against computed score contributors. Add a component regression asserting displayed score and text agree. Run UI typecheck, Vitest, build, and Playwright.\n\n## Scope and delivery\nThis item applies only to the React/Vite browser preview unless the scope decision explicitly creates a separate item. Preserve native Rust shell contracts. Implement under parent milestone BI-5u3.3; collect evidence before marking complete.\n","acceptance_criteria":"- The UI never says opportunity signals raise the score unless the formula actually uses them.\n- The formula and explanation share a single source of truth for scoring components.\n- An opportunity-only change cannot create a score-contribution claim without a corresponding score delta.\n- If a product decision adds opportunities to scoring, the weight and resulting behavior are documented and regression-tested.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#ux-07","status":"open","priority":2,"issue_type":"bug","labels":["frontend","m2","milestone","review-panel","review-remediation","ux","ux-07"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.3.4","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.3","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.3.3","title":"Use one latency severity classification across dashboard views","description":"Finding: UX-06 from the 2026-10-05 frontend UX adversarial panel.\nSource: Review report UX-06; ui/src/App.tsx:178-187,441-451,595-607; ui/src/dashboard-contract.ts:17-25; ui/src/insight-engine.ts:107-131.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n1. Apply a stage with queueDepth 0, avgLatencyMs 750, and latencyP95Ms 200. 2. Compare Job Observability and bottleneck severity. 3. Check values at, below, and above configured boundaries.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] All views derive stage severity from one shared classifier or clearly named distinct rules that cannot appear to contradict.\n- [ ] The configured latency ceiling is used consistently where the UI claims a shared status.\n- [ ] For queueDepth=0, latency=750ms, ceiling=200ms, all affected views agree on the severity.\n- [ ] Boundary behavior is deterministic and documented.\n\n## Test Plan\nUnit-test shared severity thresholds, including exact boundaries and zero queue. Component-test status parity across the affected cards. Add a regression for the panel's 750/200 example. Run UI typecheck, Vitest, build, and Playwright.\n\n## Scope and delivery\nThis item applies only to the React/Vite browser preview unless the scope decision explicitly creates a separate item. Preserve native Rust shell contracts. Implement under parent milestone BI-5u3.3; collect evidence before marking complete.\n","acceptance_criteria":"- All views derive stage severity from one shared classifier or clearly named distinct rules that cannot appear to contradict.\n- The configured latency ceiling is used consistently where the UI claims a shared status.\n- For queueDepth=0, latency=750ms, ceiling=200ms, all affected views agree on the severity.\n- Boundary behavior is deterministic and documented.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#ux-06","status":"open","priority":2,"issue_type":"bug","labels":["frontend","m2","milestone","review-panel","review-remediation","ux","ux-06"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.3.3","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.3","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.3.2","title":"Derive manager warnings and routes from active telemetry","description":"Finding: UX-05 from the 2026-10-05 frontend UX adversarial panel.\nSource: Review report UX-05; ui/src/domain/quality-pulse.ts:75-107,138-160; ui/src/App.tsx:365-390.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n1. Apply complete healthy telemetry with no high/critical bottleneck. 2. Inspect Manager recommendations and Team Lead routes. 3. Repeat with a critical stage and with no stages.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Healthy populated telemetry does not produce unconditional red critical warnings.\n- [ ] Every non-sample recommendation and route is traceable to active input records.\n- [ ] A healthy input has a clear healthy/no-action-needed state.\n- [ ] If A-124 or other canned examples remain, they are labeled as examples and are not shown as active telemetry actions.\n- [ ] Empty data has an explicit empty state rather than a fabricated fallback stage.\n\n## Test Plan\nUnit-test Quality Pulse for healthy, critical, and empty input. Component-test route/recommendation provenance and severity. Add browser cases for healthy and critical samples. Run UI typecheck, Vitest, build, and Playwright.\n\n## Scope and delivery\nThis item applies only to the React/Vite browser preview unless the scope decision explicitly creates a separate item. Preserve native Rust shell contracts. Implement under parent milestone BI-5u3.3; collect evidence before marking complete.\n","acceptance_criteria":"- Healthy populated telemetry does not produce unconditional red critical warnings.\n- Every non-sample recommendation and route is traceable to active input records.\n- A healthy input has a clear healthy/no-action-needed state.\n- If A-124 or other canned examples remain, they are labeled as examples and are not shown as active telemetry actions.\n- Empty data has an explicit empty state rather than a fabricated fallback stage.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#ux-05","status":"open","priority":2,"issue_type":"bug","labels":["frontend","m2","milestone","review-panel","review-remediation","ux","ux-05"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.3.2","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.3","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.3.1","title":"Show provenance for static audit and recommendation sections","description":"Finding: UX-02 from the 2026-10-05 frontend UX adversarial panel.\nSource: Review report UX-02; ui/src/App.tsx:704,737-744,763-768,787-788; ui/src/dashboard-content.ts:35-55.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n1. Load the preview's default/sample state. 2. Apply telemetry. 3. Compare each accessibility, SEO, security, and performance section before and after import. 4. Identify source labels.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every section identifies whether its displayed values are sample/example content or derived from the active import.\n- [ ] Imported telemetry does not make static example scores or findings appear data-derived.\n- [ ] A sample label remains visible after import when the section itself is still static.\n- [ ] The existing 'Example guidance' cue is preserved or replaced with an equally clear section-level provenance label.\n\n## Test Plan\nComponent-test provenance labels for default/sample, imported, empty, and invalid-input states. Add a Playwright assertion for the visibility of each source cue. Run UI typecheck, Vitest, production build, and Playwright.\n\n## Scope and deli…4409 tokens truncated… no task-blocking issue is reproduced, close as validated without code changes; otherwise document the reproducer and propose the smallest responsive/order fix.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#ux-13","status":"open","priority":3,"issue_type":"spike","labels":["frontend","m4","milestone","review-panel","review-remediation","ux","ux-13"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.5.2","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.5","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.5.1","title":"Resolve intended audience and runtime wording for browser bridge fallback","description":"Finding: UX-12 from the 2026-10-05 frontend UX adversarial panel.\nSource: Review report UX-12; ui/src/tauri-admin.ts:146-150,182-188; ui/codemap.md:3-7,16-26.\n\n## Goal\nResolve whether the React browser preview is a compatibility harness or a user-facing dashboard, then ensure the browser fallback wording gives accurate expectations and a usable next action.\n\n## Findings\n[DISPUTED] Panel reviewers disagreed on whether Tauri-specific bridge wording is misleading in the browser preview. The panel did not establish the intended audience or copy contract. Keep the issue validation-only until product records that decision.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n[ ] A short decision note names the preview audience and whether it is a compatibility harness or user-facing dashboard.\n[ ] Browser fallback copy accurately describes the current context and gives a usable next step.\n[ ] Native shell copy and behavior remain unchanged unless separately scoped.\n[ ] If current text is intentional, document rationale and close without code changes; if not, draft precise copy and open a scoped follow-up PR.\n\n## Test Plan\nAdd or adjust unit tests for the chosen browser fallback wording. Exercise the browser no-runtime path and confirm it does not suggest unavailable actions. No code change is required if the decision validates current text.\n\n## Scope and delivery\nThis item applies only to the React/Vite browser preview unless the scope decision explicitly creates a separate item. Preserve native Rust shell contracts. Implement under parent milestone BI-5u3.5; collect evidence before marking complete.","acceptance_criteria":"- A short decision note names the preview audience and whether it is a compatibility harness or user-facing dashboard.\n- Browser fallback copy accurately describes the current context and gives a usable next step.\n- Native shell copy and behavior remain unchanged unless separately scoped.\n- If current text is intentional, document rationale and close without code changes; if not, draft precise copy and open a scoped follow-up PR.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#ux-12","status":"open","priority":3,"issue_type":"spike","labels":["frontend","m4","milestone","review-panel","review-remediation","ux","ux-12"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.5.1","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.5","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.4.5","title":"Add navigable page headings, main landmark, and tab relationships","description":"Finding: UX-11 from the 2026-10-05 frontend UX adversarial panel.\nSource: Review report UX-11; ui/src/App.tsx:249-268,280,315,342,733-745; installed MUI Typography mapping.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n1. Inspect the rendered DOM/accessibility tree. 2. Navigate by main landmark and headings. 3. Select each SEO tab and verify associated panel.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] The main dashboard content is in one main landmark.\n- [ ] Headings express a meaningful descending structure rather than mapping major sections to repeated h6 levels.\n- [ ] Each tab is keyboard-operable and has a stable aria-controls relation to a tabpanel with matching labelledby.\n- [ ] No duplicate IDs or hidden panels exposed as active content.\n\n## Test Plan\nTesting Library asserts main, heading levels, tab roles, selected state, and ID relationships. Playwright checks keyboard tab selection. Perform a screen-reader heading/landmark spot check. Run UI typecheck, Vitest, build, and Playwright.\n\n## Scope and delivery\nThis item applies only to the React/Vite browser preview unless the scope decision explicitly creates a separate item. Preserve native Rust shell contracts. Implement under parent milestone BI-5u3.4; collect evidence before marking complete.\n","acceptance_criteria":"- The main dashboard content is in one main landmark.\n- Headings express a meaningful descending structure rather than mapping major sections to repeated h6 levels.\n- Each tab is keyboard-operable and has a stable aria-controls relation to a tabpanel with matching labelledby.\n- No duplicate IDs or hidden panels exposed as active content.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#ux-11","status":"open","priority":3,"issue_type":"bug","labels":["frontend","m3","milestone","review-panel","review-remediation","ux","ux-11"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.4.5","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.4","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":0,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.5","title":"Validation gate — Confirm preview audience and responsive task fit","description":"Milestone scope: Validation gate — Confirm preview audience and responsive task fit\n## Success Criteria\nUX-12 audience/contract wording is resolved and UX-13 has viewport/task evidence. Close either finding without code change if its concern is not reproduced; open a separate scoped PR only if validation demonstrates a user-facing defect.\n\n## Test Plan\nProduct owner records the browser preview audience and bridge contract. Run Playwright at 320, 360, 768, 1024, and 1280 CSS px plus 200% zoom for sample load, import, recommendation reading, and admin/baseline status. Check no horizontal page overflow, clipped controls, lost focus, or task-blocking order. Preserve screenshots/results as artifacts.\n\n## Delivery\nAfter this milestone passes, create a Conventional Commit slice on a clean branch/worktree based on the latest main. Open a focused PR; merge only after required checks pass at the same head. Do not include unrelated Rust/native-shell work.\n","acceptance_criteria":"UX-12 audience/contract wording is resolved and UX-13 has viewport/task evidence. Close either finding without code change if its concern is not reproduced; open a separate scoped PR only if validation demonstrates a user-facing defect.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md","status":"open","priority":3,"issue_type":"milestone","labels":["frontend","m4","milestone","review-remediation","ux"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.5","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3","type":"parent-child"},{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.5","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.4","type":"blocks"}],"dependency_count":1,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.1.4","title":"Do not label absent bottlenecks as imported data","description":"Finding: PR #114 review comment “Hide the sample bottleneck for no-data imports.”\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n1. Import an empty payload or a payload without stages.\n2. Inspect the Quality Pulse Top bottleneck metric and Top Bottleneck explainability trace.\n3. Confirm neither presents the sample fallback stage as imported telemetry.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Empty/partial imports represent an absent top bottleneck as unavailable; they do not substitute the sample “review” label.\n- [ ] The Quality Pulse metric and explainability trace both show an explicit unavailable state and identify that no bottleneck records are available.\n- [ ] Sample mode retains its current sample fallback behavior.\n- [ ] Existing imported bottleneck names remain unchanged.\n\n## Test Plan\n- Unit-test buildQualityPulse with empty and partial imported inputs and with sample fallback enabled.\n- Unit-test explainability text for absent and present bottleneck inputs.\n- Component-test the empty/partial import metric label.\n- Add a Playwright assertion for the empty-import metric and explainability trace.\n- Run TypeScript, Vitest, production build, Playwright discovery, and hosted same-head UI/aggregate checks.","acceptance_criteria":"- Empty/partial imports show the top bottleneck as unavailable, never the sample “review” label. - Explainability states that no bottleneck records are available for imported input. - Sample mode and populated imported bottleneck names remain unchanged. - Unit/component/browser tests cover empty, partial, sample, and populated modes.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#ux-05","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"bug","labels":["frontend","milestone","pr-1","review-remediation","ux","ux-05"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.1.4","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.1","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":1,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.1.5","title":"Represent absent imported top-risk data as unavailable","description":"Finding: PR #114 review comment “Mark the top-risk trace unavailable when commits are absent.”\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n1. Import an empty payload or a partial payload without commits.\n2. Inspect the Top Risk Commit explainability trace.\n3. Confirm the trace does not display a blank identifier or present missing records as low risk.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Empty/partial imported data with no commit-risk records shows an explicit unavailable summary, not a blank identifier.\n- [ ] The trace states that no commit-risk records are available and uses a neutral unavailable status, not a good/low-risk status.\n- [ ] Sample mode and populated imported commit IDs retain their current behavior.\n\n## Test Plan\n- Unit-test Quality Pulse for empty imports, partial imports with commits, and sample mode.\n- Unit-test explainability text and status for absent and present risk records.\n- Component-test the empty-import trace.\n- Add a Playwright assertion for the no-commit import trace.\n- Run TypeScript, Vitest, production build, Playwright discovery, and hosted same-head UI/aggregate checks.","acceptance_criteria":"- Empty/partial imports with no commit-risk records show Top Risk Commit as Unavailable, not a blank value. - Explainability says no commit-risk records are available and uses a neutral unavailable status. - Sample mode and populated imported commit IDs remain unchanged. - Unit/component/browser tests cover empty, partial, sample, and populated data.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#ux-05","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"bug","labels":["frontend","milestone","pr-1","review-remediation","ux","ux-05"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.1.5","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.1","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":1,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.1.6","title":"Do not mark absent risk and stage trends as healthy","description":"Finding: PR #114 review comment “Distinguish absent telemetry from healthy trends.”\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n1. Import an empty payload or a partial payload without commit or stage observations.\n2. Inspect the PR Risk Trajectory and Bottleneck Pressure trend lines.\n3. Confirm missing observations are not represented as green zero/healthy results.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Without commit-risk records, the PR Risk Trajectory value is Unavailable with neutral status and explicit no-record copy.\n- [ ] Without bottleneck records, Bottleneck Pressure is Unavailable with neutral status and explicit no-record copy.\n- [ ] When records exist and none are risky/pressured, the cards continue to show observed zero/healthy results.\n- [ ] Sample-mode results remain unchanged.\n\n## Test Plan\n- Unit-test dashboard visuals for empty, partial, healthy populated, and high-risk/pressured data.\n- Component-test empty and partial import trend values and visual status.\n- Add a Playwright assertion for empty-import risk and bottleneck trends.\n- Run TypeScript, Vitest, production build, Playwright discovery, and hosted same-head UI/aggregate checks.","acceptance_criteria":"- No commit-risk records produce an Unavailable neutral PR Risk Trajectory, not a green zero/healthy result. - No bottleneck records produce an Unavailable neutral Bottleneck Pressure, not a green zero/healthy result. - Populated healthy records still show observed healthy zero counts. - Sample mode remains unchanged; unit/component/browser tests cover these states.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#ux-05","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"bug","labels":["frontend","milestone","pr-1","review-remediation","ux","ux-05"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.1.6","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.1","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":1,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.1.8","title":"Derive populated import action routes from active telemetry","description":"PR #114 review follow-up: a complete populated imported payload currently has every stakeholder action route suppressed as Awaiting telemetry. Routes must be grounded in actual imported records without sample fallbacks.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n1. Import a complete payload containing commits, stages, and signals.\n2. Inspect Quality Pulse > Action Routing for each stakeholder.\n3. Confirm populated imports do not show Awaiting telemetry when relevant records exist.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Populated imported lead routes derive from commit IDs, levels, scores, and observed reasons.\n- Manager routes derive from stage names/statuses and distinguish healthy observed stages.\n- Executive routes derive from opportunity titles and scores.\n- Security routes derive from imported dependency/automation signals or explicitly report none.\n- Routes without relevant observations stay Awaiting telemetry and do not invent actions.\n- Sample mode retains current routes.\n\n## Test Plan\n- Unit-test complete populated imports, absent/partial inputs, and sample mode for all audiences.\n- Component-test populated route rendering for all audiences, no-data behavior, and absence of sample IDs/actions.\n- Extend Playwright import coverage for populated commit, stage, opportunity, and security route data.\n- Run pinned UI typecheck, Vitest, production build, Playwright discovery, and hosted same-head UI/aggregate checks.","acceptance_criteria":"Populated imported lead routes derive from commit IDs, levels, scores, and observed reasons. Manager routes derive from stage names/statuses and distinguish healthy observed stages. Executive routes derive from opportunity titles and scores. Security routes derive from imported dependency/automation signals or explicitly report none. Routes without relevant observations stay Awaiting telemetry and do not invent actions. Sample mode retains current routes. Unit, component, and browser tests cover populated, absent/partial, and sample inputs for all audiences.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#phase-1","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"bug","labels":["frontend","milestone","pr-1","review-remediation","ux"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.1.8","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.1","type":"parent-child"}]}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.1.9","title":"Preserve security signals outside imported result display limits","description":"Follow-up to PR #114 security review comment PRRT_kwDOSr9EN86pURV3. The imported result display can truncate commit-risk records before security classification. Classification must use every validated record, while rendered cards remain bounded.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n1. Create a valid imported payload with more commit-risk records than limits.risks.\n2. Place a security-relevant record after that limit and a non-security record beyond it.\n3. Import and inspect the full security count, recommendation, action route, displayed risk-card count, and Security panel details.\n4. Include more than three security records and confirm the panel reports the number it omits.\n\n## Test Plan\nUnit-test hidden security and non-security records. Component-test full-set counts, recommendations, and routes with limits.risks set to 1; verify displayed risk cards stay capped, the Security panel renders no more than three details, and its overflow count is accurate. Run TypeScript, full Vitest, production build, Playwright discovery, and hosted same-head CI/security/dependency review.","acceptance_criteria":"Security-signal presence, counts, recommendations, and routes derive from the complete validated imported dataset, independent of limits.risks. A security record beyond the risk-card limit is counted, recommended, and routed; a hidden non-security record does not increase security counts. The ordinary risk cards remain capped. The Security panel renders at most three details and reports the omitted matching-signal count. Empty, sample, imported, malformed-input, payload, and native-shell contracts remain unchanged. Unit/component tests cover these cases.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#phase-1","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"bug","labels":["frontend","milestone","pr-1","review-remediation","security-signal","ux","ux-followup"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.1.9","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.1","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":1,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0,"close_reason":"Implemented and independently reviewed on PR #114 code head a334cf900b61f5273d4005a05cdf71b79e7cc6b7. TypeScript, 13-file/84-test Vitest suite, production build, and diff checks passed locally. Hosted aggregate CI 37419778717, Security/RustSec/CodeQL/secret scan 37419778678, and Dependency Review 37419778673 all passed."}
{"_type":"issue","id":"BI-5u3.1.10","title":"Prioritize critical manager stages before action truncation","description":"PR #114 review follow-up: manager recommendations and the manager action route currently select the first two critical/high stages in payload order. A critical bottleneck later in the import can be hidden behind earlier high stages.\n\n## Steps to Reproduce\n1. Import at least three actionable stages.\n2. Put two high-impact/high-severity stages before a critical stage in payload order.\n3. Inspect the manager recommendation and manager action route.\n4. Confirm the critical stage is selected before truncation to two actions.\n\n## Test Plan\nAdd a regression with two high stages preceding a later critical stage and assert the critical stage appears first in both manager outputs. Verify impact ordering and deterministic ties. Run TypeScript, focused and full Vitest, production build, then hosted same-head CI and review-thread validation.","acceptance_criteria":"Critical stages rank ahead of high stages before manager recommendation or route truncation. Higher impact wins within equal severity; ties resolve deterministically. The manager recommendation and manager route both include the later critical stage when earlier high stages appear first in input. Sample fallback and lead, executive, and security outputs remain unchanged. Unit regressions cover both manager outputs, impact ordering, and deterministic ties.","spec_id":"docs/roadmap/remediation-roadmap-2026-10-05.md#phase-1","status":"closed","priority":2,"issue_type":"bug","labels":["frontend","manager-route","milestone","pr-1","review-remediation","ux"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"BI-5u3.1.10","depends_on_id":"BI-5u3.1","type":"parent-child"}],"dependency_count":1,"dependent_count":0,"comment_count":0,"close_reason":"Implemented and independently reviewed on PR #114 code head a334cf900b61f5273d4005a05cdf71b79e7cc6b7. TypeScript, 13-file/84-test Vitest suite, production build, and diff checks passed locally. Hosted aggregate CI 37419778717, Security/RustSec/CodeQL/secret scan 37419778678, and Dependency Review 37419778673 all passed."}
